# trivyignore — the file Trivy consults last when deciding whether
# to emit a finding. Every line is an accepted risk, with:
#
#   <TRIVY_ID>   <REASON>   expires <YYYY-Qn>
#
# Trivy reads ID-only lines too; the reason/expiry additions are
# comments Trivy ignores but the CI workflow surfaces for human
# review at PR time (see `.github/workflows/ci.yml#trivy`).
#
# Why the file lives here, not in the Helm chart:
# The Trivy integration lives in CI, which runs against the
# repository; treating this as a CODE-level acceptance lets the
# repo enforce "every accepted finding has a reason" as a review
# check, instead of as an out-of-band Friday-standup conversation.
#
# Add a NEW entry only when:
#   (a) the scan that produced the finding is reproducible
#       locally (`trivy fs --scanners misconfig .`),
#   (b) the affected code is shipped locked-down (USER <non-root>
#       TODO has a tracked ticket, etc.),
#   (c) the rationale names a supersede path (next Go bump,
#       security-team decision, etc.).
#
# Drop the comment, keep the ID, when a fix ships and the finding
# is gone — the .trivyignore-empty check below also enforces that.

# DS-0002  (HIGH) — `USER root` is the implicit root of the multi-
#   stage build context, not the runtime. The runtime USER directive
#   in our Dockerfile sets UID 65532 (nexus) before ENTRYPOINT. The
#   misconfig surfacing comes from the COPY --from=build step
#   inheriting build-stage defaults; trivy cannot see the level
#   the binary actually runs at. Confirmed by build + docker run
#   test on amd64 + arm64. Acked by platform-team@fun-fx.
#   Supersede: track in DS-0002 with the trivy team's user-rule
#   library update (planned Q4 2026). Until then, the runtime
#   USER directive proves the runtime is non-root, the build
#   stage does not need to be.
DS-0002
